<rml>
<head>
	<title>VaCuus M5 Material Decorators</title>
	<style>
		body
		{
			display: block;
			font-family: LatoLatin;
			font-size: 20px;
			width: 100%;
			height: 100%;
			color: #E8E8E8;
		}
		div { display: block; position: absolute; }
		.head { font-size: 24px; color: #FFD24A; }
		.cell
		{
			width: 380px;
			height: 240px;
			background-color: #10141AC0;
			padding: 10px;
			line-height: 30px;
		}
		#translucent { decorator: shader( spike-translucent ); }
		#additive    { decorator: shader( spike-additive ); }
		#opaque      { decorator: shader( spike-opaque ); }
		#mid         { decorator: shader( spike-mid ); }
		#anim        { decorator: shader( spike-anim ); }
		.box  { box-sizing: border-box; color: #FFFFFF; padding: 12dp 16dp; line-height: 24dp; font-size: 18dp; }
		.tag  { font-size: 16dp; color: #B8C4D0; }
		.shader { decorator: shader( demo-box ); }
		.mbox   { decorator: material-box( demo-box, 48dp, 0.5 ); }
		.mbord  { decorator: material-box( demo-box, 48dp, 0.5, border ); }
	</style>
</head>

<!-- THE MATERIAL-DECORATOR CANVAS (Task 5b, the production tier; the spike's injection
     surface is retired). Each cell's material is a `decorator: shader(spike-*)` key the
     vacuus.M5MatSpike toggle registers through a real UVaCuusStyleSet before this
     document loads, so the keys resolve on their first compile. Decorators paint the
     cell's box UNDER its text (RmlUi paints decoration before content) — the recorded
     z-order, where the spike injected over everything. The document is deliberately
     STATIC: after the settle, every publish it makes is the forced-republish remedy
     keeping the Time-driven #anim cell moving, and with vacuus.MaterialForcedRepublish
     0 that cell FREEZES between publishes — the spec §2(f) fact, still observable. -->
<body>
	<div style="left: 60px; top: 40px;" class="head">VaCuus M5 // MATERIAL DECORATORS</div>

	<div style="left: 60px; top: 96px;">TRANSLUCENT under text</div>
	<div style="left: 60px; top: 130px;" class="cell" id="translucent">
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
	</div>

	<div style="left: 520px; top: 96px;">ADDITIVE under text</div>
	<div style="left: 520px; top: 130px;" class="cell" id="additive">
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
	</div>

	<div style="left: 980px; top: 96px;">OPAQUE (replaces the cell box)</div>
	<div style="left: 980px; top: 130px;" class="cell" id="opaque">
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
	</div>

	<div style="left: 1440px; top: 96px;">MID base (params are game-side, UE-native)</div>
	<div style="left: 1440px; top: 130px;" class="cell" id="mid">
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
	</div>

	<div style="left: 60px; top: 426px;">TIME-ANIMATED (the freeze subject; the remedy keeps it moving)</div>
	<div style="left: 60px; top: 460px; width: 740px;" class="cell" id="anim">
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
		The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog 0123456789.
	</div>

	<!-- THE SAME demo-box material (Content/Demo, saved with 5.6) on rectangles and squares.
	     shader() stretches its corners with the cell (a square turns into a circle-ish blob, a
	     rectangle into an ellipse); material-box keeps them at 48dp x 0.5 = 24dp at every size,
	     as Slate's DrawAs=Box does. The fill gradient reads slot 4, continuous across the slice.
	     Everything here is in dp, like the corners, so the cells scale with them (a px layout
	     would hold still while the corners follow the dp ratio). The 30dp square is smaller
	     than two corners, so they squish (Slate does too). -->
	<div style="left: 880dp; top: 426dp;">SAME MATERIAL: shader(demo-box) vs material-box(demo-box, 48dp, 0.5)</div>

	<div style="left: 880dp; top: 462dp;" class="tag">RECTANGLES</div>
	<div style="left: 880dp; top: 490dp; width: 260dp; height: 120dp;" class="box shader">shader()</div>
	<div style="left: 1170dp; top: 490dp; width: 260dp; height: 120dp;" class="box mbox">material-box</div>
	<div style="left: 1460dp; top: 490dp; width: 260dp; height: 120dp;" class="box mbord">..., border</div>
	<div style="left: 1750dp; top: 490dp; width: 90dp; height: 240dp;" class="box mbox">tall</div>
	<div style="left: 880dp; top: 640dp; width: 840dp; height: 90dp;" class="box mbox">material-box, wide: the same 24dp corners</div>

	<div style="left: 880dp; top: 752dp;" class="tag">SQUARES</div>
	<div style="left: 880dp; top: 780dp; width: 160dp; height: 160dp;" class="box shader">shader()</div>
	<div style="left: 1070dp; top: 780dp; width: 160dp; height: 160dp;" class="box mbox">160</div>
	<div style="left: 1260dp; top: 780dp; width: 96dp; height: 96dp;" class="box mbox">96</div>
	<div style="left: 1386dp; top: 780dp; width: 48dp; height: 48dp;" class="mbox"></div>
	<div style="left: 1464dp; top: 780dp; width: 160dp; height: 160dp;" class="box mbord">border</div>
	<div style="left: 1654dp; top: 780dp; width: 30dp; height: 30dp;" class="mbox"></div>
	<div style="left: 1654dp; top: 820dp; width: 220dp;" class="tag">30dp: smaller than two corners, they squish</div>

	<div style="left: 60px; top: 760px; width: 740px;" class="cell" id="control">
		CONTROL CELL - no material is ever drawn here; if this text changes between
		two screenshots, the diff is not the material path.
	</div>
</body>
</rml>
